\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces The modular grid. A record locks when its position $r_p$ falls within $0.05$ of an integer node. Because \texttt  {nearest} is clamped to a minimum of $1$, every record with $r_p < 0.5$ is compared against node $1$ and can never satisfy the tolerance. That boundary sits at exactly half a modular period, at every register --- Equation~\ref {eq:floor}.}}{20}{figure.4.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4.3}Form C --- Preprocessing-Induced Concentration}{20}{section.4.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Form C. In raw form the half-period block sits below the floor and contributes no locks. A per-sector additive shift translates the whole block; if it lands on a node, a substantial fraction of the domain locks at once. Different sectors carry different offsets and light different registers.}}{20}{figure.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
